window.SKIM_PAPERS["2308.09124"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "linear-relational-embedding", "name": "Linear relational embedding (LRE)", "summary": "An affine function LRE(s) = W_r s + b_r that maps a subject's hidden representation to the object representation for one fixed relation r. The paper claims transformer LMs implicitly implement such functions for a subset of the relations they know.", "explanation": "A relational fact is a triple (subject, relation, object), such as (Miles Davis, plays the instrument, trumpet). For a fixed relation r, the paper models the LM's decoding of that relation as a single affine map: LRE(s) = W_r s + b_r, where s is the LM's hidden state at the last token of the subject at some intermediate layer l_r, and the output approximates o, the last-layer hidden state whose decoding gives the first token of the object. The form follows the linear relational embedding scheme of Paccanaro and Hinton (2001), extended with a bias term. The claim is deliberately partial: the full computation from s to o, written o = F(s, c) for a context c that evokes the relation, is highly non-linear, and the paper argues only that for some relations it is well-approximated by this affine map. In experiments on GPT-J across 47 relations, roughly 48 percent admit an LRE that reproduces the model's own predictions for a majority of subjects, while others - notably relations whose range is names of people or companies, such as company CEO - are predicted accurately by the LM yet no linear approximation of any tested kind recovers them. The layer l_r matters: the approximation holds at intermediate layers and collapses at late layers when the prompt still has relation text remaining, which the paper attributes to representations switching from carrying subject attributes to carrying next-token predictions.", "sourceNote": "Sections 2.2 and 3.1, Equations 1 and 4; heterogeneity findings in Section 4.1 and Figure 3; layer dependence in Section 4.3.", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "ownerPaper": "2308.09124", "sources": {"paperId": "2308.09124", "note": "Sections 2.2 and 3.1, Equations 1 and 4; heterogeneity findings in Section 4.1 and Figure 3; layer dependence in Section 4.3.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "relation-jacobian", "name": "The relation Jacobian as a first-order approximation", "summary": "The weight matrix of an LRE is obtained not by training a probe but by differentiating the LM itself: W is the Jacobian dF/ds of the model's subject-to-output computation on a prompt expressing the relation.", "explanation": "Let F(s, c) be the transformer's computation from the subject representation s (last subject token, intermediate layer) to the final-layer output representation o, within a context c that evokes the relation. A first-order Taylor expansion of F around a reference subject s_0 gives F(s, c) approximately equal to F(s_0, c) + W(s - s_0) = Ws + b, where W = dF/ds is the Jacobian and b = F(s_0, c) - W s_0. Both terms come from a single forward and backward pass on one prompt; no parameters are fit by optimization. This construction only makes sense if F behaves near-linearly in s over the relation's domain, which is exactly the hypothesis the paper tests rather than assumes. One systematic error is acknowledged: layer normalization is applied to s on the way in and to o on the way out, so the transformer does not pass changes of input scale through to output scale, and the raw Jacobian underestimates the magnitude of change in F even when its direction is right. Measured along paths between subject representations, the true change exceeds the Jacobian's estimate by factors of roughly 2.5 to 4.3 depending on the relation. The remedy is a scalar beta greater than 1, fixed once per model (2.25 for GPT-J), giving the final estimator F(s, c_r) approximately equal to beta W_r s + b_r.", "sourceNote": "Section 3.1, Equation 2 (Taylor expansion) and Equation 4 (beta correction); underestimation measurements in Appendix C, Table 5.", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "ownerPaper": "2308.09124", "sources": {"paperId": "2308.09124", "note": "Section 3.1, Equation 2 (Taylor expansion) and Equation 4 (beta correction); underestimation measurements in Appendix C, Table 5.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "mean-jacobian-estimation", "name": "Averaging Jacobians over examples", "summary": "Rather than trusting the Jacobian at one prompt, W and b are estimated as means over n examples of the same relation, which averages out example-specific non-linear noise.", "explanation": "A Jacobian taken at a single subject reflects the local behavior of F at that point, which may be idiosyncratic. The paper instead defines W as the expectation over example pairs (s_i, c_i) of dF/ds evaluated at each, and b as the expectation of F(s, c) - (dF/ds)s at each, with the expectation taken over n examples of the same relation (n = 8 in the experiments). The justification is a noise model: write F(s, c) = b + Ws + epsilon(s), where epsilon is a non-linear error term assumed to have zero mean and zero mean derivative over the distribution of subjects. Under that assumption, averaging the per-example Jacobians and biases gives an unbiased estimate of the shared linear component while the example-specific deviations cancel. Practically, when computing the Jacobian for one example, the remaining n - 1 examples are prepended to the prompt as few-shot demonstrations, so that the LM is steered toward producing the relation's object rather than some other plausible continuation. Reported results average over 24 trials with distinct random draws of the n examples. The averaged estimator outperforms a linear regression trained on the same n examples at matching the model's own predictions, which the paper takes as evidence that the mean Jacobian captures the model's actual decoding computation rather than merely fitting input-output pairs.", "sourceNote": "Section 3.1, Equation 3; zero-mean noise assumption formalized in Appendix B, Equations 9-13; implementation details (n = 8, few-shot prompts, 24 trials) in Section 4.", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "ownerPaper": "2308.09124", "sources": {"paperId": "2308.09124", "note": "Section 3.1, Equation 3; zero-mean noise assumption formalized in Appendix B, Equations 9-13; implementation details (n = 8, few-shot prompts, 24 trials) in Section 4.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "faithfulness-and-causality", "name": "Faithfulness and causality as validation criteria", "summary": "An estimated LRE is judged by two tests: whether it reproduces the model's own next-token predictions (faithfulness), and whether inverting it yields edits to the subject representation that change the model's output as predicted (causality).", "explanation": "Faithfulness asks whether the affine map is a good stand-in for the transformer's computation: for a held-out subject, the LM's decoder head D is applied both to the true output F(s, c) and to [[linear-relational-embedding|LRE]](s), and the test succeeds when the two argmax tokens agree (only the first token of the object is compared). Causality asks the stronger question of whether the linear structure is actually used by the model rather than merely correlated with its behavior. Given a second subject s' whose object under the relation is o', the LRE is inverted to compute an edit direction Delta-s = W_r-dagger (o' - o), where W_r-dagger is a low-rank pseudoinverse of rank rho_r; adding Delta-s to s and rerunning the model counts as a success if the model now predicts o'. The full inverse is avoided because small singular values of W dominate it and wash out the meaningful directions, so only singular values above a rank cutoff are kept; causality rises with rank up to an optimum rho_r and falls beyond it. The paper reports a strong correlation (R = 0.84 on GPT-J) between faithfulness and causality across relations when the per-relation hyperparameters - the extraction layer l_r and the rank rho_r - are chosen by grid search for best causal influence, and notes that this agreement weakens when hyperparameters are instead tuned for faithfulness. The oracle comparison for the edit is direct substitution of s' in place of s, and the LRE edit approaches that upper bound on relations where the approximation is good.", "sourceNote": "Section 3.2, Equations 5-8; derivation of the edit in Appendix D.1, Equations 18-19; low-rank inverse rationale in Appendix D.2, Equation 20; hyperparameter selection in Appendix E; correlation in Figure 6.", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "ownerPaper": "2308.09124", "sources": {"paperId": "2308.09124", "note": "Section 3.2, Equations 5-8; derivation of the edit in Appendix D.1, Equations 18-19; low-rank inverse rationale in Appendix D.2, Equation 20; hyperparameter selection in Appendix E; correlation in Figure 6.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "attribute-lens", "name": "The attribute lens", "summary": "A probing tool that applies a relation's LRE followed by the decoder head to hidden states at every layer, showing what the model knows about a subject's attribute rather than what token it will emit next.", "explanation": "The logit lens visualizes a hidden state h by decoding it directly with the LM's decoder head as D(h), showing next-token information at each layer. The attribute lens inserts a relation-specific linear map first: for a relation r with estimated [[linear-relational-embedding|LRE]], each hidden state is visualized as D(LRE(h)), producing an open-vocabulary distribution over object tokens for that relation at every layer and token position. The motivation is that a high-dimensional hidden state carries many pieces of information beyond the immediate next-token prediction, and a linear relation operator can pull out one named facet of it. This makes visible where and when the model finishes retrieving a given attribute, and it can surface knowledge that never reaches the output. The paper demonstrates this on prompts engineered to make the model state falsehoods: with a false statement repeated twice before the query, or followed by the instruction Repeat exactly, GPT-J outputs the correct fact almost never (recall at 1 of 0.02 and 0.03 across 11,891 prompts per condition), yet the attribute lens applied at the last mention of the subject recovers the correct object at recall at 1 of 0.54 and 0.63 respectively. The lens is thus presented as a way to detect cases where a model's output contradicts its own internal knowledge of a subject.", "sourceNote": "Section 5, Table 3, Figure 8; positioned against the logit lens (nostalgebraist, 2020) and trained linear-shortcut probes (Belrose et al., 2023; Din et al., 2023).", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "ownerPaper": "2308.09124", "sources": {"paperId": "2308.09124", "note": "Section 5, Table 3, Figure 8; positioned against the logit lens (nostalgebraist, 2020) and trained linear-shortcut probes (Belrose et al., 2023; Din et al., 2023).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "summary": null, "reading": [], "refs": {"accessed": []}, "regions": [], "pdfPages": 0, "readerKind": "pdf", "sections": []};
